  "step_3": "The decision reason requires the individual to compute the sum of pairwise differences of outcomes (weighted by probabilities) and prefer the lottery with the more favorable (positive) sum. In this case, the weighted sum of outcome differences is zero, so the reason would be indifferent. However, the individual does not mention or apply this calculation. Instead, the individual justifies the choice based on the chance of avoiding a loss, not on any sum of differences. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason. The information considered by the individual (risk of loss, chance of zero loss) is different from the information required by the decision reason (sum of pairwise outcome differences). The justification is also inconsistent with the rule of the decision reason. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the sum of all pairwise differences of outcomes between the two lotteries. In this case, the outcomes are: Lottery A offers 5 Euros with 100% probability, and Lottery B offers 5000 Euros with 0.1% probability and 0 Euros with 99.9% probability. The pairwise difference in outcomes is |5000 - 5| = 4995. Since there is only one non-zero outcome in B, the only pairwise difference is between 5000 and 5. The sum of all pairwise differences is therefore 4995. The decision reason prefers the lottery with the more favorable sum of outcome differences. Since both lotteries have only one outcome difference (from 5 to 5000), the sum of differences is the same regardless of which lottery is considered — it is always 4995. Thus, the sum of outcome differences is identical for both lotteries, and the reason would be indifferent between them. However, the reason specifies a preference based on which lottery has the more favorable sum — implying a comparison of the total differences in outcomes. But since the absolute difference is the same, and only one pair exists, the sum is equal. Therefore, the decision reason is applicable in principle, but leads to indifference between A and B due to equal outcome differences.",
